<article class="env-lemma" id="00S7">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00S7">Lemma <span data-tag="00S7">10.134.13</span>.</a> Let $A \to B$ be a ring map. Let $S \subset B$ be a multiplicative subset. The canonical map $\mathop{N\! L}\nolimits _{B/A} \otimes _ B S^{-1}B \to \mathop{N\! L}\nolimits _{S^{-1}B/A}$ is a quasi-isomorphism. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We have $S^{-1}B = \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits _{g \in S} B_ g$ where we think of $S$ as a directed set (ordering by divisibility), see Lemma <a href="/tag/00CR" data-tag="00CR">10.9.9</a>. By Lemma <a href="/tag/08JZ" data-tag="08JZ">10.134.12</a> each of the maps $\mathop{N\! L}\nolimits _{B/A} \otimes _ B B_ g \to \mathop{N\! L}\nolimits _{B_ g/A}$ are quasi-isomorphisms. The lemma follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/07BQ" data-tag="07BQ">10.134.9</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>